The accumulator ceiling is not a width budget: bf16 holds the same total and spills nothing
2026-09-09. Desktop 2 (Phoenix, Ryzen 7 8700G), mlir-aie ironenv (v1.4.2, llvm-aie
22.0.0.2026090201). NO NPU SESSION was built and no hardware context was taken; this reads two
already-compiled kernel objects out of ~/.npu/cache and counts instructions.

Commands (from the repo root, in the ironenv):
  python tools/aie_disasm.py ~/.npu/cache/e9ed1c603d42d8bb153dfb76/matmul_i8_i32_86901378.o --regs
  python tools/aie_disasm.py ~/.npu/cache/068c3151806dff6b4ab0f349/matmul_bf16_f32_cb36b67f.o --regs

THE HYPOTHESIS UNDER TEST. results/aie/aie2_isa_static.log measured five concurrently-live
4x8x8 int8 aie::mmul accumulators as the spill-free ceiling, and results/aie/gemm_cost_model_
nest.log found the production int8 GEMM holding eight and spilling, with a 416-byte frame.
But the two dtype paths in mm.cc ask the register file for the SAME TOTAL WIDTH, so the
ceiling might be a width budget rather than a count:

    int8  matmul_i8_i32   -> matmul_vectorized_4x2_mmul   8 accumulators x 1024 bit = 8192
    bf16  matmul_bf16_f32 -> matmul_vectorized_4x4       16 accumulators x  512 bit = 8192

If the ceiling is width, bf16 should spill too, the design rule generalises, and reblocking
int8 buys nothing. If bf16 does not spill, int8's blocking is the defect. Cheapest possible
test: read both objects.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. THE TWO OBJECTS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

== C:\Users\<user>/.npu/cache/e9ed1c603d42d8bb153dfb76/matmul_i8_i32_86901378.o
  section .text.matmul_i8_i32: 144 bundles (2 full-width, 142 compressed), frame 416 B, stack refs 33
      acc registers (9): cm0 cm1 cm2 cm3 cm4 cm5 cm6 cm7 cm8
      vec registers (12): x0 x1 x2 x3 x4 x5 x6 x7 x8 x9 x10 x11
  
== C:\Users\<user>/.npu/cache/068c3151806dff6b4ab0f349/matmul_bf16_f32_cb36b67f.o
  section .text.matmul_bf16_f32: 197 bundles (2 full-width, 195 compressed), frame 64 B, stack refs 33
      vec registers (9): x0 x1 x2 x3 x4 x5 x6 x8 x10
  
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. WHAT EACH ONE PUTS ON THE STACK
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Counting operations whose address operand is [sp, ...] inside each matmul body:

    int8   12 vlda  +  12 vst      + 3 lda + 3 st        frame 416 B
    bf16    0 vlda  +   0 vst      + 15 lda + 15 st      frame  64 B

The int8 frame reconciles exactly: 12 vector spill slots at 32 bytes each is 384 B, plus 32 B
of scalar, is 416 B.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. READING
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THE WIDTH HYPOTHESIS IS REFUTED. Both kernels occupy the same total accumulator width and the
same eight of the file's nine registers, and one of them spills twelve vector values while the
other spills none at all. bf16's fifteen stack references are every one of them SCALAR -- loop
counters and pointers, which is what a 64-byte frame is. It never puts an accumulator on the
stack. So the ceiling measured earlier is not a width budget, does not generalise across
dtypes, and the design rule cannot be stated in bits.

THE FILE ITSELF IS NINE REGISTERS, ADDRESSABLE AT THREE GRANULARITIES. The bf16 object names
bml0-bml8 and bmh0-bmh8, and both objects name amll0-amll8 through amhh0-amhh8. Those are not
separate files: they are the same nine accumulators viewed as full 1024-bit registers (cm0-cm8),
as 512-bit halves (bm), and as 256-bit quarters (am). That is consistent with the nine names
aie2_isa_static.log reported and explains why it called nine a lower bound -- it had only
looked for the cm spelling.

SO INT8'S BLOCKING IS THE DEFECT, AND BF16 IS THE EXISTENCE PROOF. A blocking that fits gives a
64-byte frame and zero accumulator traffic. int8's 4x2 does not fit and pays twelve spill
round-trips, and gemm_cost_model_nest.log already located where they run: not in the
nine-bundle hardware loop, but in the 87 non-loop bundles of the accumulator-group body, which
executes 16 times per kernel call at the n=64 tile.

WHAT THIS SUPPORTS. H11, proposed twice in this repo and never run: rebuild the int8 path on
matmul_vectorized_2x2_mmul (4 accumulators, mm.cc:81) instead of matmul_vectorized_4x2_mmul
(8, mm.cc:242). Four is under the five this repo measured as spill-free, so the prediction is a
frame that falls toward bf16's 64 B and twelve vector spills that disappear, traded against
twice as many accumulator groups per call. Whether that trade is net positive is exactly what
the cost model can answer from the rebuilt object, before any hardware runs.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT THIS DOES NOT SHOW
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- It does not explain WHY int8's eight 1024-bit accumulators overrun a nine-register file while
  bf16's sixteen 512-bit ones do not, when both occupy eight registers' worth. The int8 path
  also stages its int32 output tile through accumulator quarters (vlda amll/amhh from the C
  pointer), which bf16 does at half the width, and that is the obvious suspect -- but it is a
  suspect, not a measurement. Nothing here counts live ranges.
- Two objects, one tile geometry each, one optimisation level, one compiler version.
- The 416-byte reconciliation assumes 32-byte vector spill slots. That matches, but the slot
  size was inferred from the arithmetic rather than read from a frame layout.
- No hardware was involved and no timing is claimed. The consequence of the spill is measured
  elsewhere (gemm_cost_model_nest.log); this log only establishes that it is a blocking
  problem rather than a width limit.
